export const L8={title:'Research ethics and the allocation of scarce resources',hook:'One kidney becomes available and several people on the waiting list are compatible. Elsewhere, a state legislature debates whether to fund prenatal care or a new trauma center. Both are allocation decisions, but one concerns an individual patient and the other an entire population. Bioethics gives a vocabulary and history for reasoning about both.',objectives:['Trace modern research ethics from Nuremberg to the Belmont Report.','Apply respect for persons, beneficence and justice to research and care.','Distinguish macroallocation from microallocation.','Compare utilitarian, egalitarian and need-based allocation criteria.','Describe how organ allocation and crisis standards of care attempt fairness.'],sections:[
['Lessons that shaped research ethics',[
'Modern research ethics grew from abuse. After World War II, the **Nuremberg Code** (1947) responded to experiments by Nazi physicians and established that voluntary consent of the human subject is absolutely essential. The World Medical Association’s **Declaration of Helsinki** (1964, revised repeatedly) set guidance for physicians conducting research. In the United States, the **U.S. Public Health Service Untreated Syphilis Study at Tuskegee** (1932–1972) followed hundreds of Black men with and without syphilis without informed consent and withheld effective treatment even after penicillin became standard. Public exposure in 1972 led to the National Research Act and a federal commission.',
'That commission produced the **Belmont Report** (1979), which names three principles: **respect for persons** (treat individuals as autonomous agents and protect those with diminished autonomy, applied through informed consent), **beneficence** (do not harm and maximize possible benefits while minimizing harms, applied through risk-benefit assessment) and **justice** (fair distribution of the burdens and benefits of research, applied through fair selection of subjects). The story of **Henrietta Lacks**, whose cells were taken during cancer treatment in 1951 without her knowledge and became the widely used HeLa cell line, raised lasting questions about consent for tissue use, privacy and benefit sharing with families. Federal regulations, often called the Common Rule, require **Institutional Review Boards (IRBs)** to review research with human subjects, and extra protections apply to vulnerable groups such as children, prisoners and pregnant people.',
match('Match the Belmont principle to its application',[['Respect for persons','Informed consent'],['Beneficence','Assessment of risks and benefits'],['Justice','Fair selection of research subjects']])]],
['Research consent and the role of the IRB',[
'Research consent must explain that the activity is research, its purpose, duration and procedures, foreseeable risks and benefits, alternatives, confidentiality protections, whom to contact, and that participation is voluntary and may stop at any time without penalty. **Therapeutic misconception**—believing a study is designed primarily to benefit the individual participant—should be addressed directly, because research aims to produce generalizable knowledge. Placebo controls, randomization and blinding must be explained in plain language. An IRB can approve, require changes to or disapprove a study and continues oversight while it runs.',
order('Key events in research ethics',['Nuremberg Code responds to wartime experiments (1947)','Declaration of Helsinki guides physician researchers (1964)','Tuskegee study is publicly exposed and ended (1972)','National Research Act creates a federal ethics commission (1974)','Belmont Report names three principles (1979)'])]],
['Macroallocation and microallocation',[
'**Macroallocation** decides how much of a society’s resources go to health care and to which programs, usually by legislatures, government agencies, insurers and large organizations. Funding prenatal clinics versus a trauma center, or setting Medicaid eligibility, are macroallocation decisions. Individual patients and bedside clinicians are generally not the decision-makers at this level. **Microallocation** decides which individual patients receive a scarce resource, such as an intensive care bed, a donated organ or a limited drug supply. Microallocation is usually guided by clinicians, committees and institutional or national policies.',
'Rationing exists in every health system, whether openly through explicit rules or implicitly through price, waiting lists and geography. Ethics asks whether the criteria are relevant, transparent, consistently applied and open to appeal. Criteria based on social worth, wealth, race or celebrity are widely rejected. Medically relevant factors—likelihood of benefit, urgency, time waiting and compatibility—are more defensible.',
sort('Macro or micro?',['Macroallocation','Microallocation'],[['A legislature sets the state health budget',0],['An insurer decides which services a plan covers',0],['A hospital chooses which patient gets the last ICU bed',1],['A transplant team offers a liver to a matched candidate',1],['A government funds vaccine programs over new hospitals',0],['A pharmacy distributes a scarce drug among current patients',1]])]],
['Competing theories of fairness',[
'A **utilitarian** approach aims to produce the greatest good for the greatest number, for example by maximizing lives or life-years saved. Measures such as **quality-adjusted life years (QALYs)** combine length and quality of life to compare interventions; critics argue they can disadvantage people with disabilities or older adults. An **egalitarian** approach emphasizes equal claims, using methods such as lottery or first-come, first-served, though queues can favor those with easier access. A **need-based** or **prioritarian** approach gives priority to the sickest or worst-off. Real policies usually combine these values and document why.',
'Apply each theory to a last intensive care bed. A utilitarian team asks which patient is most likely to survive to discharge and gain the most life-years. An egalitarian team might use a lottery between patients with similar prognoses so that no one’s life is valued more than another’s. A need-based team gives the bed to the patient who will die soonest without it. Each choice protects something important and risks something else: the utilitarian rule can disadvantage people with chronic illness or disability; the lottery ignores real differences in benefit; the sickest-first rule can use resources where they help least. Ethics committees therefore ask not only which principle is correct but whether the chosen criteria were set in advance, applied consistently, explained honestly and open to review.',
{t:'w',kind:'ethicslab',title:'Practice a fair-allocation decision',scenario:2}]],
['Organ allocation and crisis standards',[
'In the United States, the **National Organ Transplant Act** (1984) prohibits buying and selling organs and established the **Organ Procurement and Transplantation Network (OPTN)**, which maintains the national waiting list and allocation policies. Allocation considers factors such as blood and tissue compatibility, medical urgency, time on the waiting list, distance between donor and recipient hospitals, and pediatric status, which vary by organ. Organs are not allocated by wealth or fame. Donation depends on the gift of donors and families under the **Uniform Anatomical Gift Act**.',
'During public health emergencies, institutions may adopt **crisis standards of care**, shifting from individual-focused care to doing the most good for the population when resources are overwhelmed. Ethical frameworks for crisis standards emphasize transparency, consistent criteria, triage teams separate from the treating clinician, reassessment, protection against discrimination based on disability, age alone or other irrelevant characteristics, and continued comfort care for everyone.',
kase('One organ, several candidates',{a:{text:'A donor kidney becomes available. A local hospital board member asks the team to move a friend up the list because he has donated to the hospital. What is the ethically and legally correct response?',choices:[['Decline; allocation follows OPTN policy and medically relevant criteria','b','Correct. Wealth, influence and social worth are not allocation criteria.',true],['Agree, because donors deserve recognition','b','This would violate allocation policy and the principle of justice.',false]]},b:{text:'Two compatible candidates have similar urgency. Which factor in national policy commonly helps decide?',choices:[['Time waiting and compatibility measures defined by policy','c','Correct. Defined, transparent criteria apply equally to all candidates.',true],['Which candidate the surgeon personally likes','c','Personal preference is not a legitimate criterion.',false]]},c:{text:'The kidney is offered according to the match list. The team documents the allocation and informs the board member that policy does not permit favoritism.',end:true}})]],
['Bring the cases together',[
'The kidney decision is microallocation governed by national policy and medically relevant criteria; the budget debate is macroallocation, where public deliberation weighs prevention against rescue and population benefit against urgent individual need. In both, the Belmont-style principles help: respect for persons demands honesty about how decisions are made, beneficence asks which choice produces the most benefit, and justice asks who bears the burdens. A strong exam answer names the level of allocation, the criteria used and the fairness theory behind them.']]
],takeaways:['Nuremberg, Helsinki, Tuskegee and Belmont shaped consent, IRB review and fair subject selection.','Macroallocation sets budgets and programs; microallocation decides which individual receives a scarce resource.','Defensible allocation uses transparent, medically relevant criteria—never wealth, fame or social worth.'],terms:[['Macroallocation','Society-level decisions about how health resources are distributed.'],['Microallocation','Decisions about which individual patients receive a scarce resource.'],['IRB','Committee that reviews and monitors research with human subjects.']],mcqs:[q('Which entities would NOT typically make macroallocation decisions?','Individual patients at the bedside','Legislatures','Government health agencies','Insurers setting coverage','Macroallocation occurs at policy and system levels.'),q('The Belmont Report’s three principles are:','Respect for persons, beneficence and justice','Autonomy, fidelity and veracity','Duty, breach and causation','Nonmaleficence, efficiency and loyalty','These three principles guide research ethics.'),q('Informed consent in research applies which Belmont principle?','Respect for persons','Justice','Beneficence','Utility','Consent respects autonomous choice.'),q('The Tuskegee study is an example of:','Research without informed consent that withheld effective treatment','A model of ethical research','An early organ allocation system','A Good Samaritan case','Its exposure led to federal reforms.'),q('Which body reviews research involving human subjects?','Institutional Review Board','Licensing board','EEOC','OPTN','IRBs approve and monitor research.'),q('A utilitarian allocation approach seeks to:','Maximize total benefit, such as lives or life-years saved','Treat whoever arrives first regardless of outcome','Distribute by social worth','Avoid all rationing','Utilitarianism focuses on aggregate outcomes.'),q('The National Organ Transplant Act:','Prohibits buying and selling organs and created the OPTN','Requires every person to donate organs','Lets hospitals sell organs to the highest bidder','Governs workplace safety','NOTA banned organ sales in 1984.'),q('Therapeutic misconception is a participant’s belief that:','Research is designed primarily for their personal benefit','Research has no risks','The IRB is the sponsor','Consent cannot be withdrawn','Researchers must address this misunderstanding.')],frq:{prompt:'A hospital has one ventilator and two patients who need it. Explain whether this is macro- or microallocation, compare two ethical approaches to deciding, and describe what makes an allocation process fair.',rubric:[['Identify microallocation',['microallocation','individual','scarce']],['Compare at least two approaches',['utilitarian','egalitarian','need','lottery','benefit']],['Describe fair process features',['transparent','criteria','consistent','appeal','triage team']],['Reject irrelevant criteria and maintain care',['wealth','social worth','disability','comfort','discriminat']]],model:'Choosing which patient receives the single ventilator is microallocation because it concerns individual patients and a specific scarce resource. A utilitarian approach would give the ventilator to the patient most likely to survive and benefit, maximizing lives or life-years saved. An egalitarian approach might use a lottery or first-come order to honor equal claims, while a need-based approach would prioritize the sicker patient. A fair process uses transparent, medically relevant criteria decided in advance, applies them consistently through a triage team separate from the treating clinicians, allows reassessment and appeal, and documents the decision. It rejects wealth, social worth, race or disability unrelated to benefit, and ensures the other patient continues to receive the best available care and comfort.'},sources:[RULES,BELMONT,CDC_TUSK,OPTN]};

export const L9={title:'Genetics, genetic engineering and reproductive issues',hook:'A woman learns from a genetic test that she carries a variant linked to breast cancer and worries her employer or insurer will find out. A couple undergoing in vitro fertilization asks about testing embryos. A news story describes babies born after gene editing. Genetics and reproduction raise questions about privacy, consent, discrimination and what kinds of changes humans should make.',objectives:['Explain genetic testing, counseling and the duty to protect genetic privacy.','Describe GINA’s protections and gaps.','Distinguish somatic gene therapy from germline editing and summarize the ethical debate.','Describe ethical and legal issues in contraception, sterilization, assisted reproduction and prenatal testing.','Recognize that reproductive law varies by state and analyze cases with neutral reasoning.'],sections:[
['Genetic testing and counseling',[
'Genetic tests can diagnose a condition, identify carriers, predict risk of later disease, guide medication choices and screen newborns. Results can affect relatives, who share genetic information, which creates questions about whether and how to inform family members. **Genetic counselors** help people understand the purpose, limits and possible results of a test before it is done and interpret results afterward, including **variants of uncertain significance**. Consent for genetic testing should cover what the test can and cannot show, how results will be shared and stored, and the possibility of unexpected findings such as misattributed parentage. Direct-to-consumer tests raise additional concerns about accuracy, interpretation without counseling, and data use by companies.',
'Every state runs **newborn screening** programs that test infants for treatable conditions such as phenylketonuria and congenital hypothyroidism; consent and storage rules for leftover blood spots vary by state and have been the subject of lawsuits. Testing can also produce **incidental findings**, such as a disease-related variant found while testing for something else. Professional groups recommend discussing before testing whether the patient wants to learn incidental findings, and laboratories may report certain actionable results. Testing minors for adult-onset conditions that have no childhood treatment is generally discouraged, so that the child can decide as an adult whether to know.']],
['Genetic privacy and GINA',[
'The **Genetic Information Nondiscrimination Act of 2008 (GINA)** prohibits health insurers from using genetic information to set eligibility or premiums, and prohibits employers (generally with 15 or more employees) from using genetic information in hiring, firing, promotion or pay decisions, or from requesting it except in narrow circumstances. GINA does **not** cover life insurance, disability insurance or long-term care insurance, and some state laws add protections. Genetic information is also health information under HIPAA, so privacy and security rules apply when it is held by covered entities.',
sort('Is it covered by GINA?',['Protected by GINA','Not covered by GINA'],[['An employer refuses to hire based on a BRCA result',0],['A health insurer raises premiums due to family history',0],['A supervisor requests employees’ genetic test results',0],['A life insurer asks about genetic test results',1],['A long-term care insurer uses a genetic test result',1],['A disability insurer considers genetic information',1]])]],
['Genetic engineering: therapy, enhancement and germline editing',[
'**Somatic gene therapy** changes genes in a patient’s body cells to treat disease; changes are not passed to children. Several gene therapies are now approved for specific conditions. **Germline editing** changes eggs, sperm or embryos, so changes would be inherited by future generations. Tools such as **CRISPR** made editing much easier. The ethical debate centers on safety (off-target effects and unknown long-term consequences), consent of future generations who cannot agree, justice (access only for the wealthy), and the line between treating disease and **enhancement** of traits. Many countries prohibit or restrict heritable editing, and international scientific bodies have called for strict limits. Other topics in this category include **cloning** (reproductive cloning is widely opposed) and **stem cell research**, where embryonic stem cells raise moral questions about embryos that adult and induced pluripotent stem cells partly avoid.',
match('Match the genetics term',[['Changes body cells; not inherited','Somatic gene therapy'],['Changes eggs, sperm or embryos; inherited','Germline editing'],['Gene-editing tool that made editing easier','CRISPR'],['Using genetics to improve normal traits rather than treat disease','Enhancement'],['A result whose health meaning is not yet known','Variant of uncertain significance']])]],
['Reproductive issues',[
'**Contraception** decisions belong to the patient, and minors’ access depends on state law and specific programs. **Sterilization** requires careful informed consent because it is intended to be permanent; federally funded sterilization has specific consent and waiting-period rules adopted after documented coercive sterilizations of poor women, women of color and people with disabilities. **Assisted reproductive technology** includes in vitro fertilization (IVF), donor eggs or sperm, and **surrogacy**; ethical issues include the status of unused embryos, consent from donors, the legal parentage of children, and the commercialization of surrogacy, where state laws differ widely. **Prenatal and preimplantation genetic testing** can detect certain conditions; disability advocates raise concerns that selection decisions may express negative judgments about people living with those conditions, while others emphasize reproductive autonomy.',
'**Abortion** law in the United States changed in 2022 when the Supreme Court’s *Dobbs v. Jackson Women’s Health Organization* decision returned regulation of abortion to the states, so legality, gestational limits and exceptions now vary substantially by state. Health professionals must know the law where they practice, provide accurate information and emergency care, and respect patients regardless of personal beliefs. **Conscience clauses** in federal and state law allow some professionals to decline participation in certain procedures, generally while still requiring timely referral or emergency care according to the applicable rules.']],
['Reason through a genetics case',[
kase('A genetic result and a worried patient',{a:{text:'A 34-year-old employee tests positive for a BRCA variant. She asks whether her employer can find out and fire her. What is accurate?',choices:[['GINA generally prohibits covered employers from using genetic information in employment decisions','b','Correct. Employers with 15 or more employees cannot use genetic information this way.',true],['Any employer may use genetic results freely','b','GINA restricts employer use of genetic information.',false]]},b:{text:'She also plans to buy life insurance. Does GINA protect her there?',choices:[['No; GINA does not cover life insurance, though some state laws might','c','Correct. GINA’s gaps include life, disability and long-term care insurance.',true],['Yes; GINA covers every type of insurance','c','GINA covers health insurance and employment only.',false]]},c:{text:'Her genetic counselor discusses screening options, how to share the result with relatives who may wish to test, and the privacy protections and gaps that apply. The discussion is documented.',end:true}}),
'Notice how the case combines genetics, privacy law and family communication. The counselor cannot share results with relatives without permission, but can help the patient decide how to inform them. A strong answer separates what the law requires from what is ethically wise.',
'Some ethicists argue for a limited **duty to warn** relatives when a result reveals a serious, preventable risk and the patient refuses to share it. Courts and professional bodies have reached different conclusions, and HIPAA permits only narrow disclosures to avert serious threats. In practice, clinicians first spend time helping the patient share the information voluntarily, often with a family letter the patient can give to relatives, and consult ethics or legal resources before considering any disclosure without permission.']],
['Summary across genetics and reproduction',[
'These topics share recurring principles. Respect for autonomy supports informed, voluntary choices about testing, contraception and reproduction. Nonmaleficence demands caution with technologies whose long-term effects are uncertain, especially when changes are inherited. Justice asks who can access expensive services, who bears risks, and whether history of coercion should shape current safeguards. Because laws differ by state and change over time, exam answers should state the principle, identify which facts depend on jurisdiction, and avoid treating any single state’s rule as national law.',
flip('Bioethics vocabulary',[['GINA','2008 law barring genetic discrimination in health insurance and employment'],['IVF','Fertilization of eggs outside the body, followed by embryo transfer'],['Surrogacy','A person carries a pregnancy for intended parents'],['Conscience clause','Law allowing some professionals to decline certain procedures'],['Preimplantation genetic testing','Testing embryos created by IVF before transfer'],['Germline','Reproductive cells whose changes can be inherited']])]]
],takeaways:['Genetic results affect relatives; counseling should precede and follow testing.','GINA protects health insurance and employment, but not life, disability or long-term care insurance.','Somatic therapy treats the patient; germline editing is inherited and widely restricted; reproductive law varies by state after Dobbs.'],terms:[['GINA','Genetic Information Nondiscrimination Act of 2008.'],['Germline editing','Inheritable genetic changes to eggs, sperm or embryos.'],['Assisted reproductive technology','Medical techniques such as IVF that help achieve pregnancy.']],mcqs:[q('GINA prohibits genetic discrimination in:','Health insurance and employment','Life insurance','Long-term care insurance','Disability insurance','GINA’s protections are limited to these two areas.'),q('Which change would be inherited by future generations?','Germline editing of an embryo','Somatic gene therapy in liver cells','A blood transfusion','A vaccine','Germline changes pass to offspring.'),q('A genetic counselor’s main role is to:','Help people understand tests and results before and after testing','Decide whether patients may have children','Sell direct-to-consumer kits','Report results to employers','Counseling supports informed decisions.'),q('A variant of uncertain significance means:','Its effect on health is not yet known','It definitely causes disease','It is a laboratory error','It is protected by GINA only','Interpretation may change with more evidence.'),q('Why do sterilization rules include special consent safeguards?','Because of documented histories of coercive sterilization','Because sterilization is always reversible','Because it is never permitted','Because GINA requires them','Safeguards respond to past abuse.'),q('After Dobbs (2022), abortion law in the U.S. is:','Set largely by individual states and varies widely','Identical in every state','Determined by HIPAA','Governed only by hospital policy','The decision returned regulation to the states.'),q('A central ethical concern with heritable gene editing is:','Future generations cannot consent and long-term effects are unknown','It is cheaper than all other care','It requires no laboratory work','It is legally required','Safety and consent are central concerns.'),q('A conscience clause generally allows a professional to:','Decline participation in certain procedures within legal limits','Refuse all emergency care','Disclose genetic results freely','Ignore state law','Conscience protections usually still require referral or emergency care.')],frq:{prompt:'A patient with a positive genetic test result fears discrimination, and her sister asks the clinic for the result. Explain the protections and gaps under GINA and privacy law, the ethical issues about family members, and how the team should respond.',rubric:[['Explain GINA protections',['gina','employment','health insurance']],['Identify GINA gaps',['life','disability','long-term care']],['Privacy: no disclosure to sister without permission',['permission','authorization','privacy','hipaa']],['Counseling and family communication',['counsel','relatives','encourage','share']]],model:'GINA prohibits health insurers from using genetic information for eligibility or premiums and prohibits covered employers from using it in employment decisions, but it does not cover life, disability or long-term care insurance, so the patient should know those gaps. The result is protected health information, so the clinic cannot disclose it to the sister without the patient’s authorization, even though relatives may share the variant. Ethically, the team respects the patient’s autonomy and privacy while recognizing a possible benefit to relatives. A genetic counselor can help the patient decide whether and how to tell family members and explain that the sister can seek her own testing. The team documents the discussion and the patient’s decision.'},sources:[RULES,GENOME,NHGRI_EDIT]};

export const L10={title:'End-of-life issues: advance directives, dying and death',hook:'An 82-year-old patient in the ICU cannot speak. Her daughter says, “Do everything,” while her son says she never wanted machines. No one can find a written directive. End-of-life law and ethics exist to help families and clinicians make decisions that reflect the patient’s own wishes when the patient can no longer voice them.',objectives:['Describe advance directives, health care powers of attorney and POLST forms.','Explain the Patient Self-Determination Act and DNR orders.','Distinguish withholding, withdrawing, palliative care, hospice, medical aid in dying and euthanasia.','Explain legal definitions of death and the organ donation process.','Summarize landmark right-to-die cases and stages of grief.'],sections:[
['Planning ahead: advance directives',[
'An **advance directive** is a legal document that records a person’s health care wishes in case they lose decision-making capacity. A **living will** states preferences about life-sustaining treatment, such as mechanical ventilation, dialysis or tube feeding, in specified situations. A **durable power of attorney for health care** (also called a health care proxy or agent) names a person to make decisions. A **POLST** (Physician Orders for Life-Sustaining Treatment, with similar names in some states) is a medical order, signed by a clinician, for seriously ill or frail patients that travels with them across settings. An advance directive expresses wishes; a POLST turns them into actionable orders.',
'The **Patient Self-Determination Act of 1990** requires hospitals, nursing facilities, home health agencies, hospices and certain health plans that participate in Medicare and Medicaid to inform adult patients at admission of their right to make advance directives, to ask whether they have one, and to document it. Facilities may not condition care on whether someone has a directive. Patients can revoke or change a directive while they have capacity.',
'A document is only as useful as the conversations behind it. Choosing an agent means picking someone who knows the person’s values, can make hard decisions under stress and is willing to follow the person’s wishes rather than their own. People are encouraged to review directives after major life events, share copies with their agent and clinicians, and discuss what quality of life means to them, not only which machines they would accept. When no agent has been named, state law usually supplies a priority list of default surrogates, commonly starting with a guardian or spouse, then adult children, parents and siblings, though the order varies.',
match('Match the document',[['States wishes about life-sustaining treatment','Living will'],['Names a person to make health decisions','Durable power of attorney for health care'],['Signed medical order that travels with a seriously ill patient','POLST'],['Order not to attempt CPR if the heart stops','DNR order'],['1990 law requiring facilities to ask about advance directives','Patient Self-Determination Act']])]],
['Resuscitation orders and treatment limits',[
'A **Do Not Resuscitate (DNR)** order, sometimes written as DNAR or AND (allow natural death), means CPR will not be attempted if breathing or heartbeat stops. It does not mean “do not treat”: a patient with a DNR may still receive antibiotics, surgery or comfort measures. Ethically and legally, **withholding** a treatment (not starting it) and **withdrawing** it (stopping it) are generally regarded as equivalent when the treatment no longer serves the patient’s goals. Clinicians sometimes feel more uneasy stopping a ventilator than never starting it, but the patient’s goals and the benefit of the treatment are what matter. A **futility** dispute arises when clinicians believe a requested treatment cannot achieve the patient’s goals; institutions use communication, ethics consultation and defined processes rather than unilateral decisions.',
'Cultural and religious beliefs shape end-of-life decisions. Some families prefer that a senior relative receive a serious prognosis first, some traditions emphasize preserving life by every possible means, and others value a natural death at home. Respecting these preferences while honoring the patient’s own choices requires asking rather than assuming, involving chaplains or cultural liaisons when helpful, and using qualified interpreters. Clinicians should also recognize **moral distress** among staff when they believe treatment is prolonging suffering, and use debriefing and ethics support rather than silence.']],
['Palliative care, hospice, aid in dying and euthanasia',[
'**Palliative care** relieves symptoms and stress of serious illness at any stage, alongside curative treatment. **Hospice** is a type of palliative care for people who are no longer pursuing cure; under the Medicare hospice benefit, a physician certifies a life expectancy of about six months or less if the illness runs its normal course. Hospice care can be given at home or in facilities and supports the family through bereavement.',
'**Medical aid in dying** (also called physician-assisted death) allows a terminally ill, capable adult to request a prescription for medication they may self-administer to end their life. It is legal in a minority of U.S. states and the District of Columbia under strict conditions, beginning with Oregon’s Death with Dignity Act (1997). **Euthanasia**, in which a clinician directly administers a lethal agent, is illegal throughout the United States. Giving pain medication to relieve suffering that may, as an unintended effect, hasten death is generally distinguished by the **principle of double effect**: the intent is comfort, not death.',
sort('Where does each belong?',['Palliative or hospice care','Medical aid in dying','Euthanasia (illegal in the U.S.)'],[['Morphine titrated to relieve a dying patient’s pain',0],['Home support for a patient with a six-month prognosis',0],['A capable terminally ill adult self-administers prescribed medication where state law allows',1],['A clinician injects a lethal dose to end a patient’s life',2],['Symptom management during chemotherapy',0]])]],
['Death, donation and landmark cases',[
'The **Uniform Determination of Death Act**, adopted in most states, recognizes death as either irreversible cessation of circulatory and respiratory functions, or irreversible cessation of all functions of the entire brain, including the brainstem (**brain death**). A person in a **persistent vegetative state** is not brain dead: the brainstem still functions. Organ donation follows the **Uniform Anatomical Gift Act**; people may register as donors or families may authorize donation, and hospitals must notify the organ procurement organization of deaths and imminent deaths. The team caring for the patient is separate from the transplant team to avoid conflicts of interest.',
'Most deceased donation follows brain death, but **donation after circulatory death** can occur when a patient who is not brain dead has life support withdrawn according to the family’s decision and the heart stops. Strict rules separate the decision to withdraw support from any discussion of donation, and a waiting period after circulation stops is observed before organs are recovered. Living donors can give a kidney or part of a liver; they must give voluntary informed consent and are evaluated by an independent advocate. Selling organs remains illegal under federal law.',
'Landmark cases shaped the law. **Karen Ann Quinlan** (New Jersey, 1976) established that a guardian could request removal of a ventilator based on the patient’s privacy rights. **Nancy Cruzan** (U.S. Supreme Court, 1990) recognized that competent people may refuse life-sustaining treatment, including artificial nutrition, and allowed states to require clear and convincing evidence of an incapacitated patient’s wishes; the case encouraged passage of the Patient Self-Determination Act. **Terri Schiavo** (Florida, 2005) became a national dispute between a spouse and parents and showed the value of written directives and a named agent.',
order('Kübler-Ross stages of grief (often taught in this order)',['Denial','Anger','Bargaining','Depression','Acceptance'])]],
['Resolve the ICU disagreement',[
kase('Siblings disagree at the bedside',{a:{text:'No written directive is found. The daughter wants every intervention; the son says their mother never wanted machines. What should the team do first?',choices:[['Identify the legally authorized surrogate under state law and ask what the patient would have wanted','b','Correct. Substituted judgment centers the patient’s own values.',true],['Follow whichever sibling speaks loudest','b','Volume is not authority; state law defines surrogate priority.',false]]},b:{text:'The siblings share equal legal priority and still disagree. What is the next step?',choices:[['Hold a family meeting with clear prognosis information and offer an ethics consultation','c','Correct. Communication and consultation help resolve conflict about the patient’s values.',true],['Withdraw treatment immediately without discussion','c','Unilateral action without process is inappropriate.',false]]},c:{text:'At the meeting, the son shares notes from a conversation in which their mother described her wishes. The family agrees on a comfort-focused plan, and the decision and its basis are documented.',end:true}}),
'Kübler-Ross’s stages describe common emotional responses, not a fixed sequence everyone follows; people may move back and forth. Supporting families means honest prognosis, time, cultural and spiritual respect, and attention to bereavement.']]
],takeaways:['Living wills state wishes, health care agents make decisions, and POLST turns wishes into portable orders; the PSDA requires facilities to ask.','DNR means no CPR, not no care; withholding and withdrawing are ethically equivalent.','Hospice requires a prognosis of about six months or less; aid in dying is legal in some states; euthanasia is illegal in the U.S.'],terms:[['Advance directive','Legal document recording health care wishes for future incapacity.'],['Brain death','Irreversible cessation of all functions of the entire brain, including the brainstem.'],['Double effect','Comfort-intended treatment that may foreseeably but unintentionally hasten death.']],mcqs:[q('Which document names a person to make health decisions if the patient cannot?','Durable power of attorney for health care','Living will only','DNR order','Notice of Privacy Practices','The agent makes decisions for the patient.'),q('The Patient Self-Determination Act requires participating facilities to:','Inform adult patients of their right to make advance directives','Require every patient to sign a DNR','Provide euthanasia on request','Destroy directives after discharge','It was passed in 1990 after Cruzan.'),q('A DNR order means:','CPR will not be attempted if the heart or breathing stops','No treatment of any kind','The patient is in hospice','The patient is brain dead','Other treatments may continue.'),q('Medicare hospice eligibility generally requires a prognosis of:','Six months or less if the illness runs its normal course','One year or less','One month or less','No prognosis requirement','Physicians certify the expected prognosis.'),q('Which is illegal throughout the United States?','Euthanasia by direct clinician administration','Palliative care','Hospice care','Advance directives','Euthanasia differs from medical aid in dying.'),q('Brain death under the UDDA requires:','Irreversible cessation of all functions of the entire brain, including the brainstem','Any loss of consciousness','A persistent vegetative state','Absence of a written directive','A persistent vegetative state is not brain death.'),q('The Cruzan case (1990) established that:','States may require clear and convincing evidence of an incapacitated patient’s wishes','Euthanasia is legal','Hospitals own advance directives','Organ sales are permitted','It recognized the right to refuse treatment and evidentiary standards.'),q('Stopping a ventilator that no longer serves the patient’s goals is generally considered:','Ethically equivalent to never starting it','Always murder','More ethical than withholding','Prohibited by the PSDA','Withholding and withdrawing are treated alike.')],frq:{prompt:'An incapacitated ICU patient has no written directive and her adult children disagree about life support. Explain the legal and ethical framework the team should use and the steps toward a decision.',rubric:[['Identify surrogate and decision standard',['surrogate','substituted judgment','best interest','state law']],['Explain advance directive tools and PSDA',['advance directive','living will','power of attorney','psda','polst']],['Distinguish treatment options',['withdraw','withhold','palliative','hospice','dnr']],['Process: meeting, ethics consult, documentation',['meeting','ethics','consult','document']]],model:'Without a directive, the team identifies the legally authorized surrogate under state law and asks the surrogate to use substituted judgment—what the patient would have wanted—or, if her wishes cannot be known, her best interests. The case shows why tools such as a living will, a durable power of attorney for health care and a POLST matter, and the Patient Self-Determination Act requires facilities to ask about them at admission. The team explains options honestly: continuing life support, a DNR order, withdrawing treatment that no longer serves her goals (ethically equivalent to withholding), and palliative or hospice care focused on comfort. A family meeting with clear prognosis information, an ethics consultation if disagreement persists, respect for cultural and spiritual needs, and careful documentation of the decision and its basis complete the process.'},sources:[RULES,NIA_AD,NIA_HOSPICE,DONATE]};
